# Copy to .env.local and fill in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (required) — one Postgres connection string, nothing else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development (docker compose up -d):
DATABASE_URL=postgres://fwai:fwai_local_dev@localhost:5433/fwai
#
# Managed/hosted example — point at the POOLED endpoint when deploying to
# Vercel, because each serverless invocation opens its own connection:
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:6543/dbname?sslmode=require
#
# TLS is enabled automatically for any non-local host. Append
# ?sslmode=disable to force it off.

# Connections per server instance. Keep it small on serverless — the platform
# scales out by adding instances, so a big pool here multiplies fast.
# PGPOOL_MAX=3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets encryption (required if you use cloud accounts / Zoom / AI Sensy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Master key for AES-256-GCM at-rest encryption of provider credentials.
# Any long random string: openssl rand -base64 48
# CHANGING THIS MAKES EVERY ALREADY-STORED CREDENTIAL UNDECRYPTABLE.
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=your-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK_INTERVAL_MS=300000        # VM/app probes            (default 5 min)
# CLOUD_SYNC_INTERVAL_MS=300000   # cloud provider sync      (default 5 min)
# METRICS_RETENTION_DAYS=7        # vm_metrics/app_metrics pruning
# SCHEDULER_DISABLED=1            # turn the in-process scheduler off entirely
#
# NOTE the in-process scheduler above only works on a long-lived server (local
# dev, a VPS, Docker). On Vercel each invocation is short-lived and setInterval
# does not survive it, so external triggers are the ONLY automation in
# production. There are two, on purpose:
#
#   GET /api/cron/check-all   every 5 minutes   VMs, apps, alerts
#     .github/workflows/monitor-cron.yml. Vercel's Hobby plan allows at most ONE
#     cron run per day, so the entry in vercel.json ("30 3 * * *") is a daily
#     backstop for this only. Any uptime pinger substitutes for the workflow —
#     the endpoint does not care who calls it, only that the secret is correct.
#
#   GET /api/cron/openai      4x a day          OpenAI project credit checks
#     See the OpenAI Track section at the bottom of this file. Separate because
#     each check is a billable OpenAI request and must not ride a 5-minute tick.
#
# Neither schedule lives in the application: nothing here computes a time of day.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cron authentication (REQUIRED in production)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret for BOTH scheduled endpoints — /api/cron/check-all and
# /api/cron/openai. One secret, one guard (requireCronSecret in src/lib/api.ts).
# The GitHub workflow and the cron-job.org job each send it as
# "Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>"; Vercel Cron attaches it automatically
# once the variable is set on the project. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 36
#
# Those routes probe the whole fleet, send WhatsApp messages and spend OpenAI
# quota, so they are NOT safe to leave open. With NODE_ENV=production and no
# secret set they refuse with 503 rather than running for anyone who calls them.
CRON_SECRET=another-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenAI Track (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The OpenAI Track page answers one question per client project: can this
# project API key currently make a billable request, or does OpenAI report
# insufficient_quota? It does NOT read a dollar balance — no OpenAI API exposes
# one for a project key. Each check is one minimal chat completion (~2 tokens).
#
# SCHEDULE: four times a day — 10:00, 13:00, 18:00 and 22:00 Asia/Kolkata — for
# projects whose "Auto check" toggle is on. NOT on the 5-minute VM cron: each
# check is a billable request, and riding that tick cost 288 per project per day.
#
# THE SCHEDULE IS EXTERNAL CONFIGURATION, not code. Set up ONE job at
# https://cron-job.org (free; Vercel Hobby cron cannot do this — one run a day,
# ±59 minutes, UTC only):
#
#   URL       https://<your-domain>/api/cron/openai
#   Method    GET
#   Header    Authorization: Bearer <the CRON_SECRET above>
#   Timezone  Asia/Kolkata          <- set this, do not convert to UTC by hand
#   Hours     10, 13, 18, 22        <- one job covers all four
#   Minutes   0
#   Save responses: on, so a failure is diagnosable from their log
#
# A self-hosted install points the same job at its own public URL. Nothing in
# the app computes a time of day, so changing these times needs no deploy.
#
# CHECKING DOES NOT MEAN MESSAGING. The probe rate is capped in SQL
# (MIN_CHECK_INTERVAL_MS in src/lib/openai-check.ts claims each project for an
# hour, so even a misconfigured job cannot exceed 24 probes a day), and WhatsApp
# is capped separately by the per-recipient latch: one message per recipient per
# no-credit episode, silence while it continues, and a fresh message only if the
# project recovers and then runs dry again.
#
# The model that check requests. A project key can be SCOPED to a subset of
# models, so if a client's key has no access to the default the check reports
# CHECK_FAILED (never NO_CREDIT) and names this variable in the error. Set it to
# any model the stored keys are allowed to use.
# OPENAI_CHECK_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini    # default
#
# OPENAI_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS=10000     # give up on one probe   (default 10s)
#
# Point at an OpenAI-compatible gateway/proxy, or at a stub for testing
# (scripts/test-openai-check.mjs does exactly that).
# OPENAI_API_BASE=https://api.openai.com/v1
